[THOUGHT] The patient localizes maximal pain to the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint — the joint between the distal and middle phalanges. This is a critical anatomic clue. DIP joint pain after trauma raises concern for:  
- Mallet finger (extensor tendon rupture or bony avulsion), especially if there’s inability to actively extend the fingertip;  
- DIP joint sprain or ligamentous injury;  
- DIP fracture (e.g., dorsal or volar lip avulsion, or intra-articular fracture);  
- Less likely: infection (but no redness/warmth/pus makes this improbable acutely).  
Since the patient hasn’t yet been asked about active extension ability — the hallmark functional test for mallet finger — this is the most discriminating next question. Inability to actively extend the distal phalanx strongly suggests extensor tendon disruption.

[QUERY] Can you try to straighten just the very tip of your finger (the part beyond the DIP joint) on your own — without using your other hand to help? If not, does it droop downward?

